A(('p', 'Every row above reads the same way, because the text gives no identification for any of them.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus gives only the closing word, nothing more is given here. A reader who says that the bridegroom is a certain person, that the oil is a certain thing, that the lamps are a certain work, that the sleep is a certain condition or that the door is a certain moment is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text does say. The text says that the kingdom of heaven is likened unto ten virgins who took their lamps and went forth to meet the bridegroom; that five were wise and five foolish; that the foolish took no oil and the wise took oil in their vessels; that the bridegroom tarried and they all slumbered and slept; that at midnight a cry was made; that the foolish asked for oil and the wise said, “Not so”; that while they went to buy the bridegroom came, those that were ready went in with him to the marriage, and the door was shut; that the other virgins came, said “Lord, Lord, open to us,” and were answered, “I know you not”; and that Jesus said, “Watch therefore.” These statements are made by the story and by the verse that follows it, and they are the only material from which the meaning can be stated.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'Jesus gives only the closing word of 25:13, so this section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself, set beside that word. It assigns no identity to any part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Jesus says the kingdom of heaven is likened unto ten virgins who went forth to meet the bridegroom, five of whom had oil with their lamps and five of whom did not. The bridegroom tarried, a cry was made at midnight, and the ready went in with him. The door was shut, and the others were answered, “I know you not.” The meaning is in what the story shows of readiness and delay, and in the word that follows it.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the kingdom of heaven.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Readiness is prepared before the cry.** The wise “took oil in their vessels with their lamps” (25:4). What is needed was in hand before the bridegroom came.',
    '**Delay is part of the story.** “While the bridegroom tarried, they all slumbered and slept” (25:5). The coming was not at once, and the text does not fault the sleep of the wise.',
    '**Readiness cannot be borrowed at the last.** The foolish ask the wise, “Give us of your oil,” and the wise answer, “Not so” (25:8–9). When the cry comes, there is no time to supply what was not prepared.',
    '**A door can be shut.** “They that were ready went in with him to the marriage: and the door was shut” (25:10). The other virgins come afterward and are answered, “I know you not” (25:11–12).',
    '**The coming is unknown in its hour.** The close says, “Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh” (25:13).',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The people Matthew names in this scene are the disciples (24:3). They had asked about the sign of His coming and the end of the world, and had heard that no man knows the day and hour (24:36). They were meant to understand that the kingdom of heaven involves a coming whose hour is not known, that readiness is prepared beforehand, and that being unready when the door is shut cannot be put right at the door. Matthew does not say that the wise or the foolish virgins are these disciples, and Jesus does not identify them.'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the comparison. The force is in the shut door. The story gives the foolish virgins a request, a going to buy, and a knocking, and it gives them no entry. The force is also in the last word, “Watch,” which follows a story in which all ten slept. The parable asks the hearer to see that readiness is a matter of what is prepared, and that the hour of the coming is not known.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. Readiness is prepared beforehand. The hour of the coming is not known. Delay does not remove the need to be ready. A shut door is not reopened by knocking. These statements arise from the comparison, and they do not say that the virgins, the lamps, the oil, the bridegroom or the door represents anyone or anything. The oil is not made into a doctrine here: Matthew does not say what it is, where it comes from or why the foolish had none.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The ten virgins stand among the parables of Matthew 24 and 25 and beside other stories of a marriage. They are kept apart from their neighbors here.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 22:1–14', '“a certain king, which made a marriage for his son”', 'The Wedding Banquet. A king, a son, servants, guests, a garment. No bridegroom, no lamps and no shut door. It stands in its own study.'],
    ['Matt 25:14–30', '“a man travelling into a far country”', 'The Talents. A man, three servants, goods delivered, a reckoning. It follows the virgins directly and is not an explanation of them.'],
    ['Matt 24:45–51', '“a faithful and wise servant”', 'The Faithful and Unfaithful Servant. It stands just before the virgins, and its servant and lord are not the virgins or the bridegroom.'],
    ['Matt 25:31–46', '“When the Son of man shall come in his glory”', 'The Sheep and the Goats. A king on a throne and nations before him. No virgins, no lamps and no door.'],
    ['Luke 12:35–38', '“like unto men that wait for their lord, when he will return from the wedding”', 'Servants waiting for a lord from a wedding. Another setting; the servants open at once. They are not the virgins.'],
    ['Matt 7:21–23; Luke 13:25–27', '“I never knew you”; “I know you not whence ye are”', 'Words like Matthew 25:12 in other settings. They are not an explanation of 25:12, and 25:12 is not an explanation of them.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The virgins are told on the mount of Olives, after the disciples’ question of 24:3. The Wedding Banquet is told in the temple, after the question about authority (21:23; 22:1). The Talents follow the virgins in the same discourse, and the Sheep and the Goats follow the Talents. A shared discourse does not make two stories one.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The virgins have ten virgins, a bridegroom, those that sell and a door. The Wedding Banquet has a king, his son, many servants, those bidden, guests and a man without a garment. The Talents has a man travelling and three servants. No character is carried from one parable into another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The virgins move from a going out, to a delay, to a cry, to a coming, to a door shut and a refusal. The Wedding Banquet moves from a sending, to a refusal with violence, to the highways, to a man cast out. None of those movements is read into the other.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the marriage of 25:10 is not the marriage the king made for his son in 22:2, and the garment of 22:11 is not the oil of 25:3–4. Second, the words “I know you not” (25:12) are not the words “outer darkness” and “weeping and gnashing of teeth” of 22:13 or 25:30, and neither set is read into the other. Third, the lord who comes in Luke 12:36 and the bridegroom who comes in Matthew 25:10 are not the same figure in the same story.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (4:17) and sent the Twelve to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (10:5–7). The ten virgins belong to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel, and Matthew places the parable on the mount of Olives among the sayings to the disciples (24:3; 25:1).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The kingdom of heaven that Jesus likens to ten virgins is the Kingdom He announced during His earthly ministry. It is not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The virgins, the lamps, the oil, the bridegroom, the marriage and the door are not the identity of the Body of Christ, and they are not its salvation. The parable is not read as a universal picture of how any person comes to God. The taking of oil, the sleeping, the buying and the knocking are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation for anyone. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of preparation.** The wise “took oil in their vessels with their lamps” (25:4). A reader learns to prepare beforehand for what he is waiting on.',
    '**A lesson of patience.** The bridegroom “tarried” (25:5). A reader learns that a delay does not cancel what is expected.',
    '**A lesson against borrowing at the last.** The wise say, “Not so” (25:9). A reader learns that some preparation cannot be handed over when the cry comes.',
    '**A lesson of watchfulness.** Jesus says, “Watch therefore” (25:13). A reader learns to live attentive to what he has been told is coming.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The Kingdom’s marriage and the bridegroom’s coming are not turned into the Body’s hope, and the shut door is not turned into a warning laid on the Body. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling, its hope and the way it is saved is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'The kingdom of heaven is likened unto ten virgins who took their lamps and went forth to meet the bridegroom. Five were wise and took oil in their vessels; five were foolish and took none. The bridegroom tarried, and they all slumbered and slept. At midnight a cry was made, and the foolish asked the wise for oil and were answered, “Not so.” While they went to buy, the bridegroom came, the ready went in with him to the marriage, and the door was shut. The other virgins came, said “Lord, Lord, open to us,” and were answered, “I know you not.” Jesus ends with “Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh.” He gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparison says that readiness is prepared beforehand, that the hour of the coming is not known, and that a shut door is not reopened by knocking. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the virgins, the bridegroom, the oil, the lamps or the door are, or that makes taking oil or knocking a way of being saved, goes beyond what the text states.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** ten virgins go forth with lamps; five are wise and five foolish; the bridegroom tarries and all sleep; a cry is made at midnight; the foolish ask for oil and go to buy; the bridegroom comes, the ready go in and the door is shut; the other virgins knock and are answered, “I know you not”; Jesus says, “Watch therefore.”',
    '**The meaning:** readiness is prepared beforehand; the hour of the coming is not known; delay does not remove the need to be ready; a shut door is not reopened by knocking.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation of the parts; no identity for the virgins, the bridegroom, the oil, the lamps or the door; nothing carried in from the Wedding Banquet or the parables beside it.',
    '**The present lesson:** preparation, patience, no borrowing at the last and watchfulness, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

